WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.560
When cryptocurrencies emerged just over a
decade ago, the talk was of a decentralized

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.879
utopia – a way of moving money around that
avoided the usual middlemen.

00:00:10.879 --> 00:00:14.919
Today things are moving in a very different
direction.

00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:20.790
Around the world, central banks are racing
to create their own versions of digital money.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:24.240
And nowhere is this happening faster than
in China.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.190
"This is going to happen.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:34.149
And those countries that trial it and test
it early will be in an advantageous position."

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:40.309
"One very obvious negative element or motivation
is the application of surveillance and monitoring."

00:00:40.309 --> 00:00:45.910
"I think this is a global race and
we cannot afford not to be part of this effort."

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:49.969
In this video, we'll look at:
- How the rise of cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin

00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:55.619
triggered a radical rethink about what money
actually is, and who controls it.

00:00:55.619 --> 00:01:01.519
- How central bank digital currencies – or
CBDCs - could disrupt existing power dynamics

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:06.650
in the financial system
- and what all this could mean for you – the

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:07.650
consumer.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:13.580
But first – some background: You've heard
of Bitcoin, Ether, Dogecoin and the like.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:18.690
These are cryptocurrencies – designed to
allow you to pay for stuff without any government

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.940
or bank being involved

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.370
They're based on blockchain technology.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.050
A kind of digital ledger that records transactions
in a chain.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:34.250
It's hard to tamper with because doing so
leaves a trace elsewhere – potentially for

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.410
everyone to see.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:42.400
That transparency is one of its most promising
features.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.520
In the past year, backing from high-profile
figures like Elon Musk as well as established

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:53.170
payment companies like Visa and Mastercard
have given Bitcoin in particular a massive

00:01:53.170 --> 00:02:00.990
boost – sending its value shooting up,
but often swiftly back down again.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:05.960
The rapid rise of cryptocurrencies initially
left governments and regulators scrambling

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.000
to respond:

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:17.950
"There was a lot of discussion around how
this Bitcoin currency would be used for money

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.270
laundering or terrorist activities.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:28.360
People were worried and they didn't understand
that the actual technology of blockchain was

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.940
an amazing way to remove intermediation."

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.840
Meanwhile, the private sector was spotting
an opportunity.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:42.500
In 2019, none other than facebook arrived
on the scene - promising to create its own

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.250
version of digital money that wasn't volatile,
like Bitcoin.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:54.830
"I believe it should be as easy to send money
to someone as it is to send a photo."

00:02:54.830 --> 00:03:00.490
The plan: a so-called "stable coin" whose
value would be pegged to a bunch of established

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:01.490
currencies.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:06.990
The project originally known as "Libra" and
since renamed "Diem," raised a lot of eyebrows:

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:12.220
"Most central banks were really scared when
they heard about this project.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:16.980
They thought it would be a new asset that
would be adopted by two billion people and

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.730
it will compete with our domestic money."

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:26.970
The idea of a giant tech company becoming
a big player in the global financial system

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.849
wasn't the only issue governments had with
cryptocurrencies.

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:36.330
They were also worried about how widespread
adoption could affect their ability to implement

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.330
monetary policy.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:38.709
Here's why:

00:03:38.709 --> 00:03:44.349
Right now, central banks have the power to
respond to what's going on in the economy.

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:48.000
If things are sluggish, they can **lower borrowing
rates.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.910
They can even just print more money.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.410
If things are overheating, they can **raise
rates.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:59.500
They can also make rules about how much cash
private banks need to hold in reserve.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:05.180
But all this falls to pieces if people are
no longer using the traditional currencies

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.160
that central banks control.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:14.930
"If the role of a central bank is to keep
financial stability, I think this would happen

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:22.430
not by ignoring what is happening out there
but actually implementing new technologies

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:29.199
and bending them in your system, trying to
be globally competitive [with innovative solutions]

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:31.540
and give more options to the citizens."

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.170
In other words, if you can't beat 'em, join
'em.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:37.130
And that's exactly what's happening right
now.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:41.790
Over the past few months, central banks have
been racing to develop their ownforms of

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:42.790
digital currency.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:50.130
"A central bank digital currency would be
a way of, to some extent, controlling the

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:57.530
potential negative impact of having Facebook
money or Amazon money."

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:04.020
"Basically central banks are approaching us
to do some pilots and tests of how we can

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:07.270
build and design CBDCs.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:16.010
We have been working with a dozen central
banks on building such CBDC networks, which

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:21.240
involve the central banks, domestic banks
but also the payment system providers."

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:26.500
And if there's one country that has responded
especially decisively, it's China.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:33.460
It recently banned homegrown banks and payment
giants like Ant Group and Tencent from handling

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:34.460
cryptocurrencies.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:40.430
And in the meantime it's been very busy creating
its own version of digital money – one that

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:42.919
it can control.

00:05:42.919 --> 00:05:47.810
Hundreds of thousands of people in China have
already taken parts in trials of the digital

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:53.790
yuan – spending their government-issued
coins in Chinese outlets of Starbucks and

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:54.790
McDonalds.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:57.699
And next year, things are set to go a step
further.

00:05:57.699 --> 00:06:05.910
"The Chinese government already has plans
to have Chinese citizens abroad use the digital

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:11.290
yuan for transactions.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:17.750
One really interesting event we can watch
is the Beijing Olympics in 2022 because they

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:28.500
have pretty big ambitions for foreign visitors
to use these systems, participate in these

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:29.800
broader pilots."

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:35.320
The digital yuan is programmable – meaning
that it can theoretically be given an expiration

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:36.320
date.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:41.389
That way, if the economy is flagging, people
can be easily coerced into spending.

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:48.169
"I think this probably plays into the Chinese
government's plan to have consumption-driven

00:06:48.169 --> 00:06:49.169
growth.

00:06:49.169 --> 00:06:53.520
I do see a little
bit of pushback.

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.870
On the forums people have talked about: what
does this actually mean?

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:02.259
I don't have a lot of control over how I spend
my money."

00:07:02.259 --> 00:07:06.789
It also means that for the first time ever
– the government will be able to track citizens'

00:07:06.789 --> 00:07:09.770
transactions – in near real time.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:13.370
The rather confusing term being used to describe
this capability?!

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:15.750
"Controllable anonymity."

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:22.840
Controllable anonymity, to my understanding,
promises a kind of horizontal anonymity, so

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:28.199
you can think of the counter-parties that
are involved in a transaction can't necessarily

00:07:28.199 --> 00:07:30.760
access each other's personal information.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:37.880
But there is no promise or guarantee of vertical
anonymity, so any kind of user information

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.680
can be readily available for the government.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:48.400
So I think that obviously has surveillance
implications even if that's not officially

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.280
in the official guidance documents."

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:57.540
China's full-steam ahead approach comes at
an already tense time for geopolitical ties.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.560
trade conflicts, questions over Beijing's
handling of the pandemic .. not to mention

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:09.050
reports of widespread suppression of minorities
in Xinjiang are ALL putting a strain on international

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:11.389
relationships.

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:16.470
… which brings us to another crucial aspect
of this story: Power within the financial

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.500
system.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:23.290
Right now, economic sanctions are one of the
main ways of dealing with political and trade

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:24.290
conflicts.

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:27.930
- Think American sanctions on North Korea
and Iran

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:32.630
- or EU sanctions on Russia following the
annexation of Crimea

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:37.300
Sanctions are enforced by stopping financial
institutions from handling transactions linked

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:42.070
to certain individuals, assets or regimes.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:47.560
Some commentators have suggested that China's
digital yuan could enable the country to avoid

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:52.180
western sanctions by lessening its dependence
on using the US dollar.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.680
But others say those concerns are overblown..

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.270
"That's a headline.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:02.440
It's an attention grabber.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:09.310
In the very, very long-run, perhaps, but my
thinking is that whether China piloting its

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:14.920
own digital currency could eventually unseat
the role of the USD, for this to be true there

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:18.450
are a few conditions that have to be present.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:24.570
First of all the Chinse Renminbi has to actually
be a preferred currency of transaction, which

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:28.450
currently it is not, at least compared to
the USD."

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:29.560
It's a fair point.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:34.920
While there has been a lot of discussion about
the Dollar's waning dominance, it does still

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:37.000
reign supreme.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:44.450
In 2020, nearly 60% of all global foreign
exchange reserves were held in US dollars.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:48.630
China's yuan, in contrast made up just over
2 % (2.25%)

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:53.440
So, as China charges ahead, what is the rest
of the world up to?

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:59.990
A recent survey showed that 80% of the world's
central banks are exploring their own digital

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:01.330
currencies.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:06.020
Major Western powers like the United States
and the United Kingdom are in the research

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:07.980
phase.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:12.350
As for the Eurozone, the European Central
Bank recently announced a multi-year project

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:14.100
to create a digital euro.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:21.020
Along with China, South Korea and Thailand
are already testing out versions of digital

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:22.020
money.

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:28.050
Canada, Russia, Venezuela, Brazil and South
Africa are all working on development.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:34.130
Fun fact: the only country to have already
introduced a CBDC is the Bahamas!

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:37.610
"Larger central banks are even more cautious.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:48.570
But they are forming working groups, assessing
what are the benefits, risks etc for the banks.

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:53.630
And in the case of the European Central Bank
in particular, there's nothing like a bit

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:57.990
of competition to speed things up.

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:03.800
“It depends which is the country that uses
the digital currency.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:12.780
If this would be the case with China, we have
every reason to invest more in having very

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:19.650
fast a digital euro because the Chinese yuan
would be used on the biggest platforms like

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.760
Tencent, Alibaba and Mastercard.”

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.220
So the pace may differ from one country to
the next, but when it comes to the question

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:33.870
of rolling out central bank digital currencies,
it seems to be less of a question of if,

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.160
but when.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:41.350
Control over monetary policy and geopolitical
wrangling are just some reasons why central

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:44.180
banks have an interest in developing digital
currencies.

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:49.161
But from a consumer's perspective, what's
the point?

00:11:49.161 --> 00:11:58.640
"The point of central bank digital currencies
is in some ways to simplify and enhance the

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:01.780
payment systems we have today."

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:05.800
If you've ever tried to send money abroad,
you'll know that it can sometimes take an

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:08.380
age to clear transactions.

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:14.300
Proponents of CBDCs say that a new internationally
compatible system would vastly speed things

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.300
up.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:20.660
This is an especially big deal for the millions
of families who rely on money sent home from

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.620
relatives working abroad.

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:28.519
Another group that could benefit are the 1.7
billion people in the world who currently

00:12:28.519 --> 00:12:30.700
don't have a bank account:

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:40.410
"Private, commercial banks don’t have much
incentive to give lower income people bank

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:46.180
accounts, partially because they don't want
the risk of these people not being able to

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:47.180
pay back their debts.

00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:57.720
If you had a CBDC you could put in place legislation
and rules to ensure that everyone would have

00:12:57.720 --> 00:13:06.350
a bank account so from a financial inclusion
perspective, I think it would be a step forward."

00:13:06.350 --> 00:13:11.010
Legislation … rules … If you think all
this sounds like we've come full-circle, you'd

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:12.200
be right.

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:18.570
After all, the technology that underpins cryptocurrencies
like Bitcoin is designed to be decentralized

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:20.170
and anonymous.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:23.970
So where does a CBDC fit into that narrative?

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:33.660
"We basically use the blockchain infrastructure
to monitor the supply, to know who owns what

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:36.980
and to record transactions.

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:41.250
But everything related to identity would be
kept in

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:46.290
an external system, managed by the banks,
the central banks, or the payment service

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:47.290
providers."

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:50.800
"This is exactly the difference that we have
with Bitcoin.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:59.240
It will be like using your credit card, and
for a user it will ensure the anonymity but

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:04.769
this doesn't mean that it doesn't leave a
trace of the transactions."

00:14:04.769 --> 00:14:10.420
But that's the very aspect that some people
are uncomfortable with fearing that these

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:16.760
"traces" could give governments too much power
and information about citizens' spending, similar

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:20.970
to the way big tech companies already mine
users' data today

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:28.980
"The incentives for a big tech company to
issue a monetary token are very high from

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:34.760
a gathering data point of view and people
might even prefer a central bank digital currency

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:41.570
if there was a design mechanism in there that
prevented the government getting hold of personalized

00:14:41.570 --> 00:14:44.220
data on what you were spending your money
on."

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:47.270
At the end of the day, at lot of this comes
down to trust.

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:51.220
Who do YOU want to control and safeguard your
money?

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:52.220
A central bank?

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:55.130
A social media company like Facebook?

00:14:55.130 --> 00:15:01.480
OR maybe – nobody at all.The answer to that
question may well determine our future relationship

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:02.480
to money.

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.889
If you have any thoughts on this, let us know
in the comments!

00:15:05.889 --> 00:15:16.839
And if you want to see more videos like this,
you can check out our playlist, right here!

